# Codex runtime guidance

Common operating policy is installed as a managed block by `setup.sh`.
Project-specific AGENTS.md instructions remain scoped to their project.

## Tier discipline

- `--profile quick` for mechanical, low-judgment work: renames, boilerplate,
  running an existing test suite, simple lookups.
- The default profile for ordinary implementation, debugging, and
  investigation — most of the work.
- `--profile deep` only with a stated reason for why the default profile is
  insufficient for this specific task. Try raising effort within the current
  profile before reaching for a higher tier — tier-up is the last resort, not
  the first move.

## Harness coverage

- Agent's gates run as Codex native hooks (`~/.codex/hooks.json` or the plugin's
  `hooks/codex-hooks.json`), covering Bash, `apply_patch`, and MCP tools. Codex
  only runs a hook after it is reviewed and trusted with `/hooks` — installing
  the hooks file does not by itself connect Codex tools to it. An action needing
  approval is denied, not queued — ask the user explicitly, then retry. Verify
  the current host's hooks and trust state before asserting coverage.
- For an existing agent session, use the repository's session coordinator to register
  a worktree. `core/infra/codex-session.sh` launches a NEW interactive Codex session;
  do not run it merely to register the current one.
- Follow the project's AGENTS.md and rules when working in the harness itself.
  Confirm the brain MCP is registered before attempting its read/capture tools.
